module router;

// router — a declarative request router, dogfooded against the limits of v0.
//
// Every web framework a programmer arrives from — FastAPI, Flask, Express,
// Axum — is built on ONE idea: a table mapping a route to a **handler
// function**, consulted at runtime to decide which code answers a request.
// `http-service` already routes, but it routes with a hand-written `if`/`else`
// chain over integer tags: adding a route there means editing the decision
// function. That is not a router, it is a switch statement. A router stores
// its handlers as *data*.
//
// This example is that table, and it works: since ADR-0008 Tier 1 landed
// first-class non-capturing function values, a handler is an ordinary value
// (`fn(in Request) -> Int`), and since ADR-0011 landed the hash map, a path
// can be looked up in one probe. The dispatch is real — `main` resolves a
// path at runtime and calls through the resulting function value, exercising a
// native indirect call, not a branch the compiler folded away.
//
// It exists to answer a question honestly: how close can v0 get to the
// declarative router shape, and exactly where does it stop? The answer is the
// SPLIT TABLE below, and the seam it forced is this example's real finding.
//
// --- The finding: why the table is split -----------------------------------
//
// The shape every framework uses is ONE table of route records:
//
//     struct Route { path: String, handler: fn(in Request) -> Int }
//     var routes = std::array::empty();          // Array[Route]
//     std::array::push(routes, Route { ... });   // register a route
//
// v0 refuses that, and refuses it *loudly* rather than mis-compiling it:
//
//     error[T0001]: `Array[Route]` is not supported in v0
//       the growable `Array` element type must be a scalar
//       (`Int`/`Float`/`Bool`/`Str`/`String`) or a supported struct/enum,
//       not `Route`
//
// A function value is not in ADR-0012's growable-element set, so a struct
// carrying one cannot be an `Array` element. The registry therefore splits in
// two, joined by an index:
//
//   * `Map[Str, Int]` — path to handler SLOT. Growable, so routes are
//     registered at runtime, in any order, by ordinary calls (`register`).
//   * `[fn(in Request) -> Int; ROUTE_COUNT]` — the handlers themselves, a
//     FIXED array, because a fixed array's element set does admit a function
//     value.
//
// The split costs exactly what the fixed half costs: `ROUTE_COUNT` is a
// compile-time constant, so the handler set is closed — a program cannot
// accept a handler it did not name in its source. Registration order must also
// agree with the array literal by hand, which `routes()` centralizes and the
// specs pin (`every_route_dispatches_to_its_own_handler`), because nothing in
// the type system relates the two halves. That unchecked correspondence is the
// finding: it is a real invariant this example maintains with tests because v0
// cannot state it in a type.
//
// Per the project's rule — every language change discovered by dogfooding gets
// an ADR and a benchmark plan, never an ad-hoc feature to make one example
// compile — this example does NOT propose syntax. It runs, it documents the
// seam, and it is the evidence a future ADR on function-valued `Array`
// elements would be argued from.
//
// --- What is deliberately NOT here -----------------------------------------
//
// The decorator spelling (`@app.get("/users")`) needs three things v0 does not
// have and this example does not fake: decorators, method-call syntax (`app.get`
// — v0 has FREE FUNCTIONS only), and capturing closures. Registration is
// therefore a plain call, which is the tuonelang-native spelling of the same
// idea. FastAPI's other half — `async` — has no analogue either: tuonelang's
// concurrency is structured fork-join, so a server here is an accept loop, not
// an event loop. Nothing below pretends otherwise.
//
// --- Two tiers: the table is pure, the wire is not --------------------------
//
// The dispatch table above is PURE — every function spec-checked, the whole
// router exercisable inside the spec sandbox with no sockets and no effects.
// That is the part worth proving exhaustively, and the specs do.
//
// Below it sits a thin EFFECT TIER that carries the table onto a real TCP
// connection: `serve_exchange` (read a request line → dispatch → respond),
// `serve_once` (the keep-alive loop over it, which closes), and
// `live_once`/`live_report` (the loopback oracle). Effectful, so no specs
// (`R0007` keeps the sandbox pure); pinned instead by the dogfood test, which
// asserts the exact response bytes a real client receives.
//
// The seam between the tiers is one line — `route(method, path)` — and it
// needs no adapter, because the router already speaks the borrowed `Str`s a
// request line is made of. `http-service` cannot do this: its `route` takes
// pre-tagged `Int`s, so its socket shell must convert a path to a tag before
// routing, and adding a route means editing that conversion. A `Str`-keyed
// table is what keeps the wire layer this thin — the practical payoff of the
// split table, beyond the finding it documents.
//
// Both of the things this example once listed as absent have since landed,
// and neither needed a language change:
//
//   * RESPONSE BODIES. A handler returns a `Response { status, body: String }`
//     rather than a status `Int`, so it can answer with content and the wire
//     layer sends a *measured* `Content-Length`. This was correctly described
//     as a table-wide change — every handler's signature moved together,
//     because the shared signature IS the table's element type — but the
//     language already supported it: a struct carrying an owned `String` is an
//     ordinary ADR-0009 value, returned through the dispatch table's indirect
//     call under ADR-0012's owned-element rules.
//
//   * CONCURRENT SERVING. `serve_pool` forks `pool_workers()` OS threads that
//     race `accept` on one shared listener. The earlier reasoning — that
//     `par_map` is fork-join over a known task set and so cannot express an
//     accept loop — had the right premise and the wrong conclusion: the task
//     set does not have to be the requests, it has to be the WORKERS. A socket
//     descriptor is an `Int`, so the shared listener is an ordinary task
//     value, and the kernel's accept queue distributes connections without
//     needing a channel at all.
//
// Since then four more of the gaps a real deployment would hit have closed,
// and again none of them needed a language change:
//
//   * REQUEST BODIES ARE DRAINED. The server read the header block but not
//     the body, so a `POST` carrying one had its response discarded: the
//     unread bytes made the kernel send RST instead of FIN, and the client
//     saw `Connection reset by peer` for a response the server had computed
//     correctly and already written. `drain_request` now parses the request's
//     own `Content-Length` and consumes the body. This was a genuine BUG, not
//     a documented limit, and it survived because the acceptance test's POST
//     case sent no body — the gap in the test was the reason the gap in the
//     server lasted.
//
//   * REQUEST TARGETS, not just paths. `/users?page=2` and `/users/42` both
//     name the `/users` resource, and an exact-match table 404s both. The
//     target is now split into (path, query), the path matched literally
//     first and only then as (parent, parameter) — literal wins, so
//     registering `/users/new` stays reachable — and both reach the handler
//     through the unchanged shared signature.
//
//   * KEEP-ALIVE. HTTP/1.1 connections are persistent by default, so a
//     client sending a second request down the same socket used to have it
//     silently dropped. `serve_once` is now a loop over `serve_exchange`,
//     bounded by `max_keep_alive_requests()` and by a SHORT idle timeout —
//     the latter is load-bearing rather than a tuning knob: a worker parked
//     on an idle keep-alive is a worker not accepting, and with a
//     fixed-width fork-join pool that turned a 4-second test run into a
//     10-minute one.
//
//   * AN UNBOUNDED SERVER. `serve_forever` accepts and serves until the
//     process is killed. The earlier note claiming this was absent was
//     imprecise about WHY: an unbounded *loop* costs the language nothing,
//     and never did. What v0 cannot express is unbounded *concurrency* —
//     fork-join must join, so a pool cannot both serve forever and return.
//     So `serve_forever` is honestly serial: a genuine long-running server
//     (the shape deployed behind a load balancer), and not a concurrent one.
//     `serve_pool` still takes a budget, and that boundary is real.
//
// What remains genuinely absent is TLS, and it is absent by decision rather
// than oversight: ADR-0017 excludes it because it needs X.509 parsing, a
// certificate store, and AEAD ciphers on top of the handshake — a dependency
// this workspace avoids, in which even SHA-256 is hand-rolled. `std::crypto`
// having landed SHA-256/HMAC in tuonelang weakens but does not overturn that,
// so an HTTPS server is a future ADR, not a gap to paper over here.
//
// It compiles with `tuo check`, its specs run under `tuo test`, and it runs
// natively with `tuo run` — serving its six sample requests over a real
// loopback socket and exiting with `report()` (74) only when the wire agrees
// with the table.

// --- The request ------------------------------------------------------------

/// A parsed request, reduced to what the router decides on. `path` is a
/// borrowed `Str` because the router never owns it — it is a slice of the
/// request line the socket layer read.
struct Request {
    method: Int,
    path: Str,
    /// The raw query string (`page=2`), empty when the target had no `?`.
    /// Handlers read it with `query_get`.
    query: Str,
    /// The trailing path parameter (`42` in `/users/42`), empty for a route
    /// matched literally. A handler that ignores it behaves exactly as before,
    /// which is why adding it did not disturb the shared handler signature's
    /// meaning for the routes that do not take one.
    param: Str,
}

/// Method tags. v0 has no string enum, and comparing method *names* on every
/// dispatch would byte-compare a `Str` per route, so the parse layer decodes a
/// method once into a tag and the router compares integers.
fn method_get() -> Int {
    1
}
fn method_post() -> Int {
    2
}
fn method_delete() -> Int {
    3
}

/// Decode a method token into its tag (0 = unknown), the one place method
/// spellings are compared.
fn method_tag(in token: Str) -> Int {
    if token == "GET" {
        method_get()
    } else if token == "POST" {
        method_post()
    } else if token == "DELETE" {
        method_delete()
    } else {
        0
    }
}

// --- Path parsing: query strings and path parameters (items 2) --------------
//
// A request target is not a route key. `/users?page=2` and `/users/42` both
// name the `/users` resource, but neither matches the literal key `/users` in
// the registry, so both used to 404. Splitting the target into (path, query)
// and then matching a trailing segment is what turns an exact-match table into
// a router a real client can talk to. All of it is pure, so all of it is
// spec-checked.

/// The byte offset of `?` in a request target, or -1 when there is no query.
fn query_start(in target: Str) -> Int {
    var i = 0;
    var found = 0 - 1;
    let n = std::str::len(target);
    while i < n {
        if std::str::byte_at(target, i) == 63 && found < 0 {
            found = i;
        }
        i = i + 1;
    }
    found
}

/// The path half of a request target — everything before `?`.
///
/// `/users?page=2` yields `/users`, which is the key the registry holds. A
/// target with no query is returned unchanged.
fn target_path(in target: Str) -> Str {
    let q = query_start(target);
    if q < 0 {
        target
    } else {
        std::str::slice(target, 0, q)
    }
}

/// The query half of a request target — everything after `?`, exclusive.
///
/// Returns the empty string when there is no query, so a caller never has to
/// unwrap an `Option` to ask whether one was present; `query_start` answers
/// that question when the distinction matters.
fn target_query(in target: Str) -> Str {
    let q = query_start(target);
    if q < 0 {
        ""
    } else {
        std::str::slice(target, q + 1, std::str::len(target))
    }
}

/// Look up one parameter in a `a=1&b=2` query string.
///
/// Returns the empty string when the key is absent or has no value. Matching
/// is exact on the key, and scanning stops at the first match, so a repeated
/// key takes its first value — the behavior most frameworks pick.
fn query_get(in query: Str, in key: Str) -> Str {
    let n = std::str::len(query);
    let k = std::str::len(key);
    var i = 0;
    var result = "";
    var done = false;
    while i < n && !done {
        // The end of this `key=value` pair: the next `&`, or the end.
        var end = i;
        while end < n && std::str::byte_at(query, end) != 38 {
            end = end + 1;
        }
        // The `=` inside it, if any.
        var eq = i;
        while eq < end && std::str::byte_at(query, eq) != 61 {
            eq = eq + 1;
        }
        if eq - i == k {
            var same = true;
            var j = 0;
            while j < k {
                if std::str::byte_at(query, i + j) != std::str::byte_at(key, j) {
                    same = false;
                }
                j = j + 1;
            }
            if same {
                if eq < end {
                    result = std::str::slice(query, eq + 1, end);
                }
                done = true;
            }
        }
        i = end + 1;
    }
    result
}

/// The offset of the last `/` in a path, or -1 if there is none.
fn last_slash(in path: Str) -> Int {
    var i = 0;
    var found = 0 - 1;
    let n = std::str::len(path);
    while i < n {
        if std::str::byte_at(path, i) == 47 {
            found = i;
        }
        i = i + 1;
    }
    found
}

/// The parent of a path whose final segment is a parameter: `/users/42`
/// yields `/users`, which IS a registry key.
///
/// This is how a table of literal paths serves `/users/{id}` without the
/// registry learning a pattern language: strip the last segment, look the
/// parent up, and hand the stripped segment to the handler as a parameter.
/// A path with no parent segment (`/users`, `/`) yields the empty string, so
/// the caller can tell "no parameterized form exists" from a real parent.
fn parent_path(in path: Str) -> Str {
    let s = last_slash(path);
    if s <= 0 {
        ""
    } else {
        std::str::slice(path, 0, s)
    }
}

/// The final segment of a path — the parameter value in `/users/42`.
fn last_segment(in path: Str) -> Str {
    let s = last_slash(path);
    if s < 0 {
        ""
    } else {
        std::str::slice(path, s + 1, std::str::len(path))
    }
}

// --- Status codes -----------------------------------------------------------

fn status_ok() -> Int {
    200
}
fn status_created() -> Int {
    201
}
fn status_no_content() -> Int {
    204
}
fn status_not_found() -> Int {
    404
}
fn status_method_not_allowed() -> Int {
    405
}

// --- The response -----------------------------------------------------------

/// What a handler answers with: a status **and a body**.
///
/// The body is an owned `String` because a handler builds it — it is not a
/// slice of anything that outlives the call, so `Str` would have nothing to
/// borrow from. That ownership is the whole reason this struct exists rather
/// than the bare `Int` the table used to carry: an `Int` can be returned by
/// value forever, but the moment a handler *composes* its answer, something
/// has to own the bytes.
///
/// This is the change the example previously documented as absent. It is a
/// table-wide change, not a wire-layer one — every handler's signature moves
/// together, because the shared signature is exactly what makes them
/// interchangeable values. Nothing in the language needed to change: a
/// struct carrying an owned `String` is an ordinary ADR-0009 value, and
/// ADR-0012's owned-element rules already cover returning one through the
/// indirect call the dispatch table makes.
struct Response {
    status: Int,
    body: String,
}

/// A response with no body — the common case, and the one a status-only
/// route wants. Kept as a named constructor so a handler never spells the
/// empty string, and so "no body" is one decision made in one place.
fn no_body(take status: Int) -> Response {
    Response { status: status, body: std::string::empty() }
}

/// A response whose body is the given text.
fn with_body(take status: Int, in text: Str) -> Response {
    var body = std::string::empty();
    std::string::append(body, text);
    Response { status: status, body: body }
}

// --- The handlers -----------------------------------------------------------
//
// Each handler is an ordinary top-level `fn` with ONE shared signature —
// `fn(in Request) -> Response` — which is what makes them interchangeable
// values storable in a single array. This uniform signature is the router's
// real contract; a handler with a different shape could not sit in the table.
//
// The signature carries a `Response` rather than the `Int` it once did, so a
// handler can answer with content. Every handler moved at once, which is the
// cost the example named when it listed bodies as absent: the table's element
// type IS the signature, so there is no way to give one route a body without
// giving every route the capacity for one.

/// `GET /` — the service root, which answers with a short greeting so the
/// body path is exercised by the most ordinary route in the table.
fn handle_root(in request: Request) -> Response {
    if request.method == method_get() {
        with_body(status_ok(), "tuonelang router")
    } else {
        no_body(status_method_not_allowed())
    }
}

/// `GET /health` — a liveness probe, which answers with **no** body.
///
/// 204 is defined to carry no content, so this route is the one that proves
/// the body is genuinely optional: a `Content-Length: 0` here is correct,
/// while a body would make the response malformed.
fn handle_health(in request: Request) -> Response {
    if request.method == method_get() {
        no_body(status_no_content())
    } else {
        no_body(status_method_not_allowed())
    }
}

/// `GET /users` lists, `POST /users` creates — the one route in this table
/// whose answer depends on the method, so it proves the handler really
/// receives the request rather than just its path.
fn handle_users(in request: Request) -> Response {
    if request.method == method_get() {
        // `/users/42` — a path parameter selects one user. The handler reads
        // `param` exactly as a framework's `def get(id)` would, except the
        // value arrived through the shared signature rather than a generated
        // argument list.
        if std::str::len(request.param) > 0 {
            var body = std::string::empty();
            std::string::append(body, "{\"id\":\"");
            std::string::append(body, request.param);
            std::string::append(body, "\"}");
            return Response { status: status_ok(), body: body };
        }
        // `/users?page=2` — a query parameter narrows the collection. Absent,
        // `query_get` yields the empty string and the unfiltered list stands.
        let page = query_get(request.query, "page");
        if std::str::len(page) > 0 {
            var body = std::string::empty();
            std::string::append(body, "{\"page\":\"");
            std::string::append(body, page);
            std::string::append(body, "\"}");
            return Response { status: status_ok(), body: body };
        }
        with_body(status_ok(), "[\"ada\",\"grace\"]")
    } else if request.method == method_post() {
        with_body(status_created(), "{\"created\":true}")
    } else {
        no_body(status_method_not_allowed())
    }
}

/// The fallback handler, dispatched to whenever no route matches. A router
/// answering an unknown path is a normal outcome, not an error path: making
/// 404 an ordinary slot in the table means `dispatch` has exactly one exit and
/// never needs an `Option` the caller must unwrap.
fn handle_not_found(in request: Request) -> Response {
    with_body(status_not_found(), "not found")
}

// --- The split table --------------------------------------------------------

/// The number of handler slots — the FIXED half of the split table. Adding a
/// route means adding a handler, extending `handler_table`, and registering
/// the path in `routes`; the specs pin all three agreeing.
fn route_count() -> Int {
    4
}

/// The slot every unmatched path falls through to. It is the LAST slot by
/// convention, so the real routes occupy `0..3` in registration order.
fn not_found_slot() -> Int {
    3
}

/// The handler table: the fixed array a function value may live in.
///
/// This is the half ADR-0012 constrains. A `[fn(in Request) -> Int; N]` is
/// fine — a fixed array's elements may be function values — while the
/// growable `Array[Route]` this would rather be is refused. Slot order is
/// load-bearing and matches `routes()` exactly.
fn handler_table() -> [fn(in Request) -> Response; 4] {
    [handle_root, handle_health, handle_users, handle_not_found]
}

/// Register one route: bind `path` to a handler `slot`. This is the
/// tuonelang-native spelling of `@app.get("/users")` — a plain call, made
/// while the table is being built, with no decorator and no method syntax.
fn register(mut routes: Map[Str, Int], in path: Str, take slot: Int) -> Int {
    std::map::insert(routes, path, slot);
    slot
}

/// Build the route registry — the growable half of the split table.
///
/// The map is populated at RUNTIME by ordinary calls, which is the property
/// that makes this a router rather than a switch: routes are data, registered
/// in any order, and a program could register them from a config file just as
/// easily. Each slot must match `handler_table`'s index, an invariant the
/// specs check rather than the type system.
fn routes() -> Map[Str, Int] {
    var table = std::map::empty();
    register(table, "/", 0);
    register(table, "/health", 1);
    register(table, "/users", 2);
    table
}

/// Resolve a path to a handler slot, falling back to `not_found_slot()`.
///
/// One hash probe, regardless of how many routes are registered — the reason
/// the registry is a map and not a scan. `http-service`'s `if`/`else` chain
/// compares every route in turn; this does not.
fn slot_for(in table: Map[Str, Int], in path: Str) -> Int {
    match std::map::get(table, path) {
        Some { value } => value,
        None => not_found_slot(),
    }
}

/// Dispatch a request through the table: resolve its path to a slot, then
/// **call the handler stored there**.
///
/// The call through `handler` is a genuine indirect call (ADR-0008 Tier 1) —
/// the target is a value read out of an array at an index computed at
/// runtime, so no compiler pass can turn it back into a direct call. That is
/// what makes this a real dispatch table.
fn dispatch(in table: Map[Str, Int], in request: Request) -> Response {
    let slot = slot_for(table, request.path);
    let handler = handler_table()[slot as Usize];
    handler(request)
}

/// Route a raw (method, target) pair — the whole router in one call, the
/// entry point a connection handler would use after parsing a request line.
///
/// The target is a *request target*, not a bare path: it may carry a query
/// (`/users?page=2`) and it may name a subresource (`/users/42`). Resolution
/// is three steps, in the order a framework resolves them:
///
///   1. split the query off, since `?page=2` is never part of a route key;
///   2. try the path as a literal key — the common case, one hash probe;
///   3. only if that misses, try its parent with the last segment as a
///      parameter, so `/users/42` reaches the `/users` handler with
///      `param = "42"`.
///
/// Step 3 runs second on purpose: a literal route must always win over a
/// parameterized interpretation of the same path, or registering `/users/new`
/// would become unreachable the moment `/users` existed.
fn route(in method: Str, in target: Str) -> Response {
    let table = routes();
    let path = target_path(target);
    let query = target_query(target);
    let tag = method_tag(method);
    if slot_for(table, path) != not_found_slot() {
        return dispatch(table, Request { method: tag, path: path, query: query, param: "" });
    }
    let parent = parent_path(path);
    if std::str::len(parent) > 0 && slot_for(table, parent) != not_found_slot() {
        return dispatch(table, Request { method: tag, path: parent, query: query, param: last_segment(path) });
    }
    dispatch(table, Request { method: tag, path: path, query: query, param: "" })
}

/// Route a request and report only its status — the spelling every caller
/// that does not need the body wants.
///
/// The specs below use this heavily, and that is the point: adding bodies did
/// not make the status contract harder to state. A route's status is still one
/// call, so the assertions that pinned the table before still read the same
/// way, and a body regression cannot hide behind a rewritten spec.
fn status_for(in method: Str, in path: Str) -> Int {
    route(method, path).status
}

/// Route a request and report its body's length — the observable that proves
/// a body was actually built, without pinning the exact bytes in the pure
/// specs (the wire tests assert those).
fn body_len_for(in method: Str, in path: Str) -> Int {
    std::str::len(std::string::as_str(route(method, path).body))
}

// --- The acceptance oracle --------------------------------------------------

/// A synthetic traffic sample driven through the full table, summed so a
/// single integer proves every dispatch path executed: a root GET (200), a
/// health GET (204), a users GET (200), a users POST (201), an unknown path
/// (404), and a method the route refuses (405).
///
/// 200 + 204 + 200 + 201 + 404 + 405 = 1614, and the report is
/// `1614 - 1540 = 74` — a value chosen to land inside one byte so the process
/// exit status carries it exactly, with no `& 0xff` truncation to explain.
fn report() -> Int {
    let a = status_for("GET", "/");
    let b = status_for("GET", "/health");
    let c = status_for("GET", "/users");
    let d = status_for("POST", "/users");
    let e = status_for("GET", "/missing");
    let f = status_for("DELETE", "/health");
    ((((a + b) + c) + d) + (e + f)) - 1540
}

// --- The effect tier: serving the table over a real socket ------------------
//
// Everything above is pure, and stays pure — the dispatch table is the part
// worth spec-checking, and it is checked exhaustively. What follows is the
// thin effectful shell that carries it onto a wire, mirroring how
// `tuo-stdlib` and `http-service` split their tiers: no spec down here
// (`R0007` keeps the spec sandbox pure), each function marked `EFFECT:` and
// pinned by the dogfood test instead.
//
// The shell is deliberately small. Its whole job is: read a request line,
// hand the method and path to `route` as the borrowed `Str`s they already
// are, and write back what HTTP requires. The router itself needs no
// adaptation to be served — that is the point being demonstrated.

/// The bound every socket wait carries (ADR-0017), in milliseconds. Generous
/// enough that a healthy loopback round-trip never reaches it, short enough
/// that a peer which goes away fails the run instead of wedging it forever.
fn io_timeout_ms() -> Int {
    5000
}

/// How many consecutive idle `accept_timeout` intervals `serve_on` tolerates
/// before concluding its remaining clients are never coming.
///
/// `serve_on` is promised exactly `n` connections, so a single quiet interval
/// says nothing — the caller may simply be between requests. Twelve strikes at
/// `io_timeout_ms()` each is a full minute of silence, comfortably longer than
/// any pause a live caller imposes and still bounded, so a genuinely abandoned
/// server exits rather than lingering forever.
fn idle_strike_limit() -> Int {
    12
}

/// The status line for a routed status — the reverse of `method_tag`, mapping
/// the router's answer back to the text HTTP puts on the wire. Pure, so it is
/// spec-checked.
fn response_line(take status: Int) -> Str {
    if status == status_ok() {
        "HTTP/1.1 200 OK"
    } else if status == status_created() {
        "HTTP/1.1 201 Created"
    } else if status == status_no_content() {
        "HTTP/1.1 204 No Content"
    } else if status == status_not_found() {
        "HTTP/1.1 404 Not Found"
    } else {
        "HTTP/1.1 405 Method Not Allowed"
    }
}

/// Render a non-negative integer as decimal text.
///
/// Needed because `Content-Length` is now computed from the body rather than
/// being the constant zero it was when every response was empty. Pure, so it
/// is spec-checked.
fn int_to_text(take n: Int) -> String {
    var out = std::string::empty();
    if n == 0 {
        std::string::push_byte(out, 48);
        return out;
    }
    // Collect digits least-significant first, then reverse — the ordinary
    // trick, written out because v0 has no string formatting.
    var digits = std::string::empty();
    var v = n;
    while v > 0 {
        std::string::push_byte(digits, 48 + (v % 10));
        v = v / 10;
    }
    let text = std::string::as_str(digits);
    var i = std::str::len(text);
    while i > 0 {
        std::string::push_byte(out, std::str::byte_at(text, i - 1));
        i = i - 1;
    }
    out
}

/// The complete header block for a response carrying `length` body bytes: the
/// CRLF ending the status line, the **measured** `Content-Length`, a
/// `Connection: close`, and the blank line that terminates the block.
///
/// The length is computed, not assumed. That is the substantive difference
/// bodies make to the wire: a client reads exactly `Content-Length` bytes, so
/// a wrong number is worse than a missing body — it makes the client hang
/// waiting for bytes that never come, or truncate the ones it got. Pure, so
/// it is spec-checked.
fn headers_for(take length: Int, take keep_alive: Bool) -> String {
    var out = std::string::empty();
    std::string::append(out, "\r\nContent-Length: ");
    std::string::append(out, std::string::as_str(int_to_text(length)));
    if keep_alive {
        std::string::append(out, "\r\nConnection: keep-alive\r\n\r\n");
    } else {
        std::string::append(out, "\r\nConnection: close\r\n\r\n");
    }
    out
}

/// Find the first space at or after `from`, or -1 when the line has none.
/// The request line is `METHOD SP PATH SP VERSION`, so two spaces split it
/// into the three tokens the router needs. Pure, so it is spec-checked.
fn find_space(in line: Str, take from: Int) -> Int {
    var i = from;
    var found = 0 - 1;
    let n = std::str::len(line);
    while i < n {
        if std::str::byte_at(line, i) == 32 && found < 0 {
            found = i;
        }
        i = i + 1;
    }
    found
}

/// EFFECT: read one line (bytes up to CR, LF, or end of input, exclusive) off
/// a connection into an owned `String`, every byte **bounded** by
/// `read_byte_timeout` (ADR-0017) rather than blocking forever on a silent
/// peer.
///
/// `read_ok` reports whether the line ended for a legitimate reason (a CR/LF
/// delimiter or clean end of input) rather than a timeout or host error. The
/// distinction matters: a timeout that quietly returned the bytes read so far
/// would look exactly like a short-but-complete request line, and the router
/// would dispatch a truncated request as if the peer had sent it — precisely
/// the confusion ADR-0017's distinct `-3` sentinel exists to prevent.
///
/// Effectful, so no spec (`R0007`); pinned natively by the dogfood test.
fn read_line_from(take conn: Int, mut read_ok: Bool) -> String {
    read_line_within(conn, read_ok, io_timeout_ms())
}

/// EFFECT: `read_line_from` with an explicit per-byte bound.
///
/// Keep-alive needs two different bounds on the same read — the full I/O
/// timeout for a request already in flight, a short one for an idle
/// connection being held open speculatively — so the timeout became a
/// parameter rather than a constant.
///
/// Effectful, so no spec (`R0007`); pinned natively by the dogfood test.
fn read_line_within(take conn: Int, mut read_ok: Bool, take timeout_ms: Int) -> String {
    var line = std::string::empty();
    var done = false;
    read_ok = true;
    while !done {
        let b = std::rt::read_byte_timeout(conn, timeout_ms);
        if b == 13 {
            // A CR: consume the LF of the pair too, so the close stays
            // orderly and no delimiter byte is left unread.
            let lf = std::rt::read_byte_timeout(conn, timeout_ms);
            if lf < 0 && lf != 0 - 1 {
                read_ok = false;
            }
            // HTTP line endings are CRLF. A CR followed by anything else is
            // malformed, and consuming that byte unconditionally used to
            // *swallow* it: `GET / HTTP/1.1\rHost: x` lost the `H` of `Host`,
            // and on a kept-alive connection a stray CR could eat the first
            // byte of the next request's method, turning `GET` into `ET` and
            // answering 405 for a well-formed request. Report it instead, so
            // the caller closes rather than parsing a corrupted stream.
            if lf >= 0 && lf != 10 {
                read_ok = false;
            }
            done = true;
        } else {
            if b == 10 || b == 0 - 1 {
                done = true;
            } else {
                if b < 0 {
                    // -3 (timeout) or -2 (host error): the peer never finished.
                    read_ok = false;
                    done = true;
                } else {
                    std::string::push_byte(line, b);
                }
            }
        }
    }
    line
}

/// Case-insensitive ASCII byte compare — HTTP header names are defined
/// case-insensitively, so `content-length` and `Content-Length` must both
/// match. Pure, so it is spec-checked.
fn lower_byte(take b: Int) -> Int {
    if b >= 65 && b <= 90 {
        b + 32
    } else {
        b
    }
}

/// Whether `line` begins with the header name `name`, compared
/// case-insensitively and followed by a colon.
///
/// This is deliberately not `std::str::starts_with`: that compares bytes
/// exactly, and a client sending `content-length:` (entirely legal) would
/// slip past it, leaving the body unread — the precise failure this whole
/// path exists to prevent. Pure, so it is spec-checked.
fn header_is(in line: Str, in name: Str) -> Bool {
    let n = std::str::len(name);
    if std::str::len(line) <= n {
        return false;
    }
    if std::str::byte_at(line, n) != 58 {
        return false;
    }
    var i = 0;
    var same = true;
    while i < n {
        if lower_byte(std::str::byte_at(line, i)) != lower_byte(std::str::byte_at(name, i)) {
            same = false;
        }
        i = i + 1;
    }
    same
}

/// The largest body this server will accept, and the ceiling `header_int`
/// refuses to parse past.
///
/// A limit is not a shortcoming: a server with no bound on a client-declared
/// length will read whatever a peer claims to be sending, which is a memory
/// exhaustion vector even when the arithmetic is safe. 8 MiB is generous for
/// this router's routes and small enough that a hostile `Content-Length`
/// cannot make the server sit in a read loop indefinitely.
fn max_body_bytes() -> Int {
    8388608
}

/// Parse the decimal value of a header line's field-value, skipping the
/// optional whitespace HTTP allows after the colon.
///
/// Returns -1 for a value that is absent, non-numeric, malformed, or larger
/// than `max_body_bytes()`, so a caller can distinguish "no valid length"
/// from a genuine zero.
///
/// **The ceiling is a safety property, not a policy knob.** tuonelang traps
/// on integer overflow rather than wrapping, and a trap aborts the *process*.
/// Accumulating `value * 10 + digit` over an unbounded digit string therefore
/// let any peer kill the server with one header line — `Content-Length:` and
/// twenty nines was a pre-authentication remote process kill, taking down
/// every in-flight connection with it (and, under `serve_pool`, all four
/// worker threads mid-`par_map`). Stopping at the ceiling means the multiply
/// can never overflow, because `value` is bounded by `max_body_bytes()`
/// before it happens.
///
/// Scanning also stops at the first bad byte. The old loop kept going after
/// setting its `bad` flag, so a value it was *going* to reject still had to
/// overflow its way to that rejection — the rejection came too late to
/// matter. Pure, so it is spec-checked.
fn header_int(in line: Str, in name: Str) -> Int {
    if !header_is(line, name) {
        return 0 - 1;
    }
    let n = std::str::len(line);
    var i = std::str::len(name) + 1;
    while i < n && std::str::byte_at(line, i) == 32 {
        i = i + 1;
    }
    var value = 0;
    var digits = 0;
    var bad = false;
    while i < n && !bad {
        let b = std::str::byte_at(line, i);
        if b < 48 || b > 57 {
            bad = true;
        } else {
            // Check the ceiling BEFORE multiplying, so the multiply itself
            // can never overflow and trap.
            if value > (max_body_bytes() - (b - 48)) / 10 {
                bad = true;
            } else {
                value = value * 10 + (b - 48);
                digits = digits + 1;
            }
        }
        i = i + 1;
    }
    if bad || digits == 0 {
        0 - 1
    } else {
        value
    }
}

/// EFFECT: drain a header block only — every line up to the terminating
/// blank line — returning how many lines were consumed.
///
/// The client half of the oracle wants this rather than `drain_request`: it is
/// reading a *response*, whose body it then reads explicitly, so parsing a
/// request's `Content-Length` here would consume bytes the caller is about to
/// read itself.
///
/// Effectful, so no spec (`R0007`); pinned natively by the dogfood test.
fn drain_header_block(take conn: Int) -> Int {
    var draining = true;
    var lines = 0;
    while draining {
        var ok = false;
        let header = read_line_from(conn, ok);
        if !ok {
            draining = false;
        } else {
            if std::str::len(std::string::as_str(header)) == 0 {
                draining = false;
            } else {
                lines = lines + 1;
            }
        }
    }
    lines
}

/// EFFECT: drain the request's remaining header block — every line up to the
/// terminating blank line — **and then its body**, returning how many body
/// bytes were consumed.
///
/// This is correctness, not politeness. A real client (curl, a browser, any
/// HTTP library) sends `Host:`, `User-Agent:`, `Accept:` immediately after the
/// request line, and a `POST`/`PUT` follows the blank line with a body. If the
/// server answers and closes with any of those bytes still unread in its
/// receive buffer, the kernel sends **RST** instead of a graceful FIN, and an
/// RST makes the peer discard data it had already received — including the
/// response just written. The client reports a connection reset and never
/// sees an answer the server computed correctly.
///
/// Draining the headers alone is not enough, and that gap was a real bug:
/// `curl -X POST -d hello=world` got `Recv failure: Connection reset by peer`
/// while the identical POST with an empty body succeeded, because only the
/// body bytes were left behind. The body's length comes from the request's
/// own `Content-Length`, which is why `header_int` is parsed here rather
/// than the header block being skipped blindly.
///
/// A body with no `Content-Length` is not drained: without a length there is
/// nothing to read up to, and reading until timeout would stall every such
/// request by `io_timeout_ms`. Chunked transfer encoding is therefore not
/// supported, and is documented as absent rather than silently mishandled.
///
/// Effectful, so no spec (`R0007`); pinned natively by the dogfood test's
/// real-client round-trip with a body.
fn drain_request(take conn: Int) -> Int {
    var draining = true;
    var length = 0;
    var header_error = false;
    while draining {
        var ok = false;
        let header = read_line_from(conn, ok);
        if !ok {
            // The header block never terminated — a timeout or a peer that
            // went away mid-block. The stream is not at a request boundary,
            // so the connection cannot be reused.
            header_error = true;
            draining = false;
        } else {
            let raw = std::string::as_str(header);
            if std::str::len(raw) == 0 {
                draining = false;
            } else {
                if header_is(raw, "Content-Length") {
                    let declared = header_int(raw, "Content-Length");
                    if declared < 0 {
                        // Present but unparseable or over `max_body_bytes()`.
                        // There is no safe length to drain to, so the body's
                        // extent is unknown and the connection must close
                        // rather than risk reading a body as a request.
                        header_error = true;
                    } else {
                        length = declared;
                    }
                }
            }
        }
    }
    if header_error {
        return 0 - 1;
    }
    var read = 0;
    var short_body = false;
    while read < length && !short_body {
        let b = std::rt::read_byte_timeout(conn, io_timeout_ms());
        if b < 0 {
            // The peer promised more than it sent, or stalled past the bound.
            // Stop rather than block — but remember that we did, because the
            // byte stream is now mid-body and the NEXT read on this
            // connection would treat the remaining body bytes as a request
            // line. Returning -1 tells the caller the connection can no
            // longer be reused, which is the difference between a short body
            // and a forged second request.
            short_body = true;
        } else {
            read = read + 1;
        }
    }
    if short_body {
        return 0 - 1;
    }
    read
}

/// The fallback response for a request line so malformed it has no method and
/// path to route — the same 404 the table's fallback handler gives, built
/// here because there is no `Request` to dispatch.
fn route_not_found() -> Response {
    with_body(status_not_found(), "not found")
}

/// EFFECT: write a complete HTTP response — status line, measured headers,
/// then the body bytes — to a connection.
///
/// The body is written only when it is non-empty. That is not an
/// optimization: a 204 is defined to carry no content, so writing zero bytes
/// after a `Content-Length: 0` keeps the response well-formed for the one
/// route in this table that must not have a body.
///
/// `keep_alive` selects the `Connection:` header. It must agree with what the
/// server then *does* — announcing `keep-alive` and closing anyway strands a
/// client waiting for a second response on a dead socket — which is why the
/// decision is computed once in `serve_exchange` and passed down rather than
/// being re-derived here.
///
/// Effectful, so no spec (`R0007`); pinned natively by the dogfood test's
/// exact-byte assertions.
fn write_response(take conn: Int, take response: Response, take keep_alive: Bool) -> Int {
    let body = std::string::as_str(response.body);
    let length = std::str::len(body);
    std::rt::write(conn, response_line(response.status));
    std::rt::write(conn, std::string::as_str(headers_for(length, keep_alive)));
    if length > 0 {
        std::rt::write(conn, body);
    }
    length
}

/// EFFECT: serve exactly one request/response exchange on an already-accepted
/// connection, **without closing it**, dispatching through the table above
/// unchanged.
///
/// Read the request line, split it into method and target, hand both to
/// `route` as the borrowed `Str`s the router already speaks, drain the
/// request, and write a complete response. Returns the routed status, or -1
/// when no request line arrived within the bound — which on a kept-alive
/// connection is the ordinary signal that the client is done, not an error.
///
/// The line worth noticing is `route(method, target)`: the pure router is
/// called here exactly as the specs call it, with no adapter and no
/// re-encoding. `http-service` cannot do this — its `route` takes pre-tagged
/// `Int`s, so its socket shell must convert a path into a tag before routing,
/// and every new route means editing that conversion. A `Str`-keyed table is
/// what lets the wire layer stay this thin.
///
/// Effectful, so no spec (`R0007`); pinned natively by the dogfood test.
fn serve_exchange(take conn: Int, take keep_alive: Bool, take first_on_conn: Bool) -> Int {
    var read_ok = false;
    // The FIRST request on a connection gets the full I/O bound: the client
    // has just connected and is expected to be mid-write. A SUBSEQUENT one
    // gets the short idle bound, because the connection is being held open
    // speculatively and no request may be coming at all.
    var wait = keep_alive_idle_ms();
    if first_on_conn {
        wait = io_timeout_ms();
    }
    let line = read_line_within(conn, read_ok, wait);
    if !read_ok {
        return 0 - 1;
    }
    let raw = std::string::as_str(line);
    if std::str::len(raw) == 0 && !first_on_conn {
        // A blank line AFTER a request is the CRLF some clients leave
        // trailing: nothing to route, and answering it would be a spurious
        // 404. A blank line as the *first* thing on a connection is different
        // — it is a real (if empty) request the client is waiting on, and
        // dropping it silently resets the connection instead of answering.
        // That distinction was a genuine bug: treating both as end-of-input
        // turned `\r\n\r\n` from a 404 into a connection reset.
        return 0 - 1;
    }
    let first = find_space(raw, 0);
    if first < 0 {
        // No method/target split: malformed, and a router answers garbage
        // with 404 rather than failing — a real server's behavior. A
        // malformed request also ends the connection: the byte stream is no
        // longer trustworthy, so continuing to parse it would compound the
        // error rather than recover from it.
        drain_request(conn);
        write_response(conn, route_not_found(), false);
        // The response was written with `Connection: close`, so the loop must
        // actually close. Returning the 404 status here would look like an
        // ordinary served request and keep the connection open, contradicting
        // the header just sent and leaving the client reading a socket the
        // server has stopped answering.
        return 0 - 2;
    }
    let method = std::str::slice(raw, 0, first);
    let second = find_space(raw, first + 1);
    var target = std::str::slice(raw, first + 1, std::str::len(raw));
    if second > 0 {
        target = std::str::slice(raw, first + 1, second);
    }
    let response = route(method, target);
    let status = response.status;
    // `drain_request` reports -1 when it could not leave the stream at a
    // request boundary — a short or stalled body, an unterminated header
    // block, or a `Content-Length` it refused to trust. The response is still
    // correct and is still sent, but the connection must NOT be reused: the
    // unread bytes would be parsed as the next request line, so a client
    // could smuggle a forged request into its own body. `write_response`'s
    // contract is that `keep_alive` says what the server will actually do,
    // and this is the call site that has to honor it.
    let in_sync = drain_request(conn) >= 0;
    write_response(conn, response, keep_alive && in_sync);
    if !in_sync {
        // Answered, but the connection must now close.
        return 0 - 2;
    }
    status
}

/// How long a kept-alive connection waits for the client's **next** request
/// before closing.
///
/// Deliberately much shorter than `io_timeout_ms`, and for a different
/// reason. That bound protects an *in-progress* request from a peer that
/// stalls mid-write, where waiting is correct. This one bounds an *idle*
/// connection, where waiting costs a worker: with a fixed-width fork-join
/// pool, a worker parked on an idle keep-alive is a worker not accepting, and
/// the pool cannot join until every one of them is done. Holding a connection
/// open for the full I/O timeout made the pool take ten minutes to exit.
///
/// This is the keep-alive trade-off made explicit: a longer value saves more
/// handshakes, a shorter one frees workers sooner.
fn keep_alive_idle_ms() -> Int {
    250
}

/// How many requests one kept-alive connection may serve before the server
/// closes it.
///
/// A limit is not a shortcoming here, it is the standard defence: an
/// unbounded keep-alive lets one peer hold a worker forever, which with a
/// fork-join pool of fixed width is a denial of service rather than a
/// slow client. Real servers cap it the same way (nginx's
/// `keepalive_requests` defaults to 1000).
fn max_keep_alive_requests() -> Int {
    64
}

/// EFFECT: serve a whole connection — **HTTP/1.1 keep-alive**: successive
/// requests on the same socket until the client stops sending, the request
/// budget is spent, or a request is malformed — then close.
///
/// This is item 3, and it is what makes the server ordinary rather than a
/// demo: HTTP/1.1 connections are persistent by default, so a client that
/// sends two requests down one socket (curl given two URLs, any browser, any
/// pooled HTTP library) previously had its second request silently dropped
/// when the server closed after the first.
///
/// The last response is deliberately sent with `Connection: close` rather
/// than the connection simply being closed after a `keep-alive` header: a
/// client must be *told* the exchange is over, or it will hold the socket
/// open waiting for a response that is never coming, and pay a timeout for
/// it. Announcing the close is the difference between a clean end and a
/// client-visible stall.
///
/// Returns the status of the first request served, so every existing caller
/// and test that read a single status from a single connection still does.
///
/// Effectful, so no spec (`R0007`); pinned natively by the dogfood test.
fn serve_once(take conn: Int) -> Int {
    var first_status = 0 - 1;
    var served = 0;
    var serving = true;
    while serving {
        // The final permitted request is answered with `close`, so the client
        // learns the connection is ending from the response it already
        // expects rather than from a socket that stops answering.
        let last = served + 1 >= max_keep_alive_requests();
        let status = serve_exchange(conn, !last, served == 0);
        if status < 0 {
            // -2 means a response WAS written and the connection must now
            // close; -1 means no request arrived at all. Both end the loop,
            // but only the former counts as having answered, which is what
            // the caller's status reports.
            if status == 0 - 2 && served == 0 {
                first_status = status_not_found();
            }
            serving = false;
        } else {
            if served == 0 {
                first_status = status;
            }
            served = served + 1;
            if last {
                serving = false;
            }
        }
    }
    std::rt::close(conn);
    first_status
}

/// EFFECT: serve one request to ourselves over a real loopback socket and read
/// the response back the way a client does — the acceptance oracle for the
/// wire path.
///
/// Listen on an ephemeral port, connect, accept, send a complete request
/// (request line, a header, the blank line), serve it, then read the status
/// line and drain the response's header block. Returns the served status only
/// when the wire's status line equals the pure model's `response_line` and the
/// framing terminated; any divergence, timeout, or socket failure returns -1.
///
/// Every wait is bounded, so a peer that never answers ends the run instead of
/// hanging CI, and the descriptors are closed on the failure paths too.
///
/// Effectful, so no spec (`R0007`).
fn live_once(in method: Str, in path: Str) -> Int {
    let listener = std::rt::listen(0);
    if listener < 0 {
        return 0 - 1;
    }
    let port = std::rt::bound_port(listener);
    if port <= 0 {
        std::rt::close(listener);
        return 0 - 1;
    }
    let client = std::rt::connect_timeout("127.0.0.1", port, io_timeout_ms());
    if client < 0 {
        std::rt::close(listener);
        return 0 - 1;
    }
    let conn = std::rt::accept_timeout(listener, io_timeout_ms());
    if conn < 0 {
        std::rt::close(client);
        std::rt::close(listener);
        return 0 - 1;
    }
    std::rt::write(client, method);
    std::rt::write(client, " ");
    std::rt::write(client, path);
    std::rt::write(client, " HTTP/1.1\r\nHost: 127.0.0.1\r\n\r\n");
    let served = serve_once(conn);
    var reply_ok = false;
    let reply = read_line_from(client, reply_ok);
    let headers = drain_header_block(client);
    // Read whatever body follows the header block, so the oracle checks the
    // bytes a client would actually receive rather than stopping at the
    // framing. `read_line_from` stops at end of input, and the server has
    // closed by now, so this drains the body exactly.
    var body_ok = false;
    let body = read_line_from(client, body_ok);
    std::rt::close(client);
    std::rt::close(listener);
    // The body on the wire must equal the body the pure table produced for
    // the same request — the property that makes bodies real rather than
    // decorative. A 204 produces an empty one, and both sides agree on that.
    let expected = route(method, path);
    let matches = std::string::as_str(body) == std::string::as_str(expected.body);
    if reply_ok && headers == 2 && matches && std::string::as_str(reply) == response_line(served) {
        served
    } else {
        0 - 1
    }
}

/// EFFECT: the wire oracle — serve the whole synthetic traffic sample over
/// real sockets and sum it exactly as `report()` sums the pure dispatches.
///
/// This is the router's acceptance property: routing a request over TCP must
/// reach the same answer as routing it in memory. Because the sum is built
/// from the same six requests in the same order, it can only equal
/// `report()` if every route, every method-refusal, and the fallback all
/// survived the round trip through parsing, framing, and the socket.
fn live_report() -> Int {
    let a = live_once("GET", "/");
    let b = live_once("GET", "/health");
    let c = live_once("GET", "/users");
    let d = live_once("POST", "/users");
    let e = live_once("GET", "/missing");
    let f = live_once("DELETE", "/health");
    ((((a + b) + c) + d) + (e + f)) - 1540
}

/// EFFECT: bind `port` and serve `n` connections from whatever client shows
/// up, then close the listener — a real accept loop, not a self-test.
///
/// This is the entry point that makes the router a *server* rather than a
/// program that talks to itself: the peer is arbitrary, so the bytes on the
/// wire must satisfy a client this program has no control over. The dogfood
/// test drives exactly this path with its own TCP client, which is what pins
/// the response framing against a peer that did not agree to be lenient.
///
/// Returns the number of connections served. Bounded: an accept that times
/// out ends the loop rather than hanging forever.
///
/// Effectful, so no spec (`R0007`); pinned natively by the dogfood test.
fn serve_on(take port: Int, take n: Int) -> Int {
    let listener = std::rt::listen(port);
    if listener < 0 {
        return 0 - 1;
    }
    var served = 0;
    var remaining = n;
    var idle_strikes = 0;
    while remaining > 0 {
        let conn = std::rt::accept_timeout(listener, io_timeout_ms());
        if conn < 0 {
            // An accept that times out means no client has arrived *yet* —
            // not that no client ever will. This loop has a known budget
            // (`n` connections were promised), so ending it on the first
            // quiet interval is wrong: a caller that pauses between requests
            // for longer than `io_timeout_ms` would find the server already
            // gone, and its next connect refused. That is a real failure mode,
            // not a hypothetical — it is what made the dogfood test flaky,
            // since the suite compiles and links native binaries in parallel
            // and can easily leave a multi-second gap between two requests.
            //
            // Give up only after `idle_strike_limit()` consecutive quiet
            // intervals, so the loop still terminates when a client really
            // has abandoned it, but tolerates an ordinary pause. The pool's
            // worker uses the opposite policy on purpose (see `pool_worker`):
            // there an idle worker must join promptly or it delays the whole
            // fork-join, and its budget is a capacity rather than a promise.
            idle_strikes = idle_strikes + 1;
            if idle_strikes >= idle_strike_limit() {
                remaining = 0;
            }
        } else {
            idle_strikes = 0;
            let status = serve_once(conn);
            if status > 0 {
                served = served + 1;
                remaining = remaining - 1;
            }
            // A connection that never sent a request — a port scanner, a
            // health-check TCP probe, a browser preconnect — does not consume
            // the promised budget. It used to, so an unrelated probe could
            // make the server exit before answering the requests it was
            // actually given.
        }
    }
    std::rt::close(listener);
    served
}

/// Whether a socket result is the ADR-0017 **timeout** sentinel (-3) rather
/// than a host error (-2).
///
/// The distinction is the whole point of that sentinel: a timeout means "not
/// yet", a host error means "not ever", and a server that conflates them
/// either exits on its first quiet second or spins forever on a broken
/// listener. Pure, so it is spec-checked.
fn is_timeout(take result: Int) -> Bool {
    result == 0 - 3
}

/// EFFECT: serve **without a connection budget** — an accept loop that runs
/// until the process is killed, which is the shape every deployed server has.
///
/// This is item 5, and it is worth being precise about what did and did not
/// change, because the example previously documented an unbounded server as
/// absent.
///
/// What was actually absent was never the unbounded *loop* — `while true`
/// costs the language nothing. It was unbounded **concurrency**: v0's only
/// concurrency primitive is `std::rt::par_map`, structured fork-join, and
/// fork-join must join, so a pool cannot both serve forever and return. That
/// limit is real and stands; see `serve_pool`, which still takes a budget.
///
/// So this loop is honest about its shape: it is **serial** and unbounded —
/// one connection served to completion before the next is accepted, forever.
/// That is a genuine long-running server (it is how a single-threaded event
/// loop is deployed behind a load balancer), and it is not a concurrent one.
/// A slow client blocks the next, which `io_timeout_ms` bounds but does not
/// eliminate.
///
/// Unlike `serve_on` there is no idle-strike limit: a quiet interval means no
/// client has arrived *yet*, and a server with no budget has no reason to
/// conclude that none ever will. An accept error that is not a timeout does
/// end the loop, since a broken listener will not fix itself.
///
/// Never returns under normal operation, so its `Int` result exists only for
/// the listener-error path. Effectful, so no spec (`R0007`); pinned natively
/// by the dogfood test, which runs it and kills it.
fn serve_forever(take port: Int) -> Int {
    let listener = std::rt::listen(port);
    if listener < 0 {
        return 0 - 1;
    }
    var running = true;
    var served = 0;
    while running {
        let conn = std::rt::accept_timeout(listener, io_timeout_ms());
        if conn < 0 {
            // -3 is a timeout: no client yet, which is not a reason to stop.
            // Anything else means the listener itself is broken.
            if !is_timeout(conn) {
                running = false;
            }
        } else {
            serve_once(conn);
            served = served + 1;
        }
    }
    std::rt::close(listener);
    served
}


/// Exit with the router's report: 74 — but only after the table has proved
/// itself on a real socket.
///
/// `main` computes the report twice: once through the pure dispatch table, and
/// once by serving the identical six requests to itself over loopback TCP. It
/// exits 74 only when the two agree, so the documented exit byte is now
/// evidence that the router *serves*, not merely that it dispatches. A
/// framing or parsing regression that left the pure specs green would break
/// the agreement and exit 0, which the dogfood test reports loudly.
// --- Concurrent serving (ADR-0007 + ADR-0015) -------------------------------
//
// The example previously listed concurrent serving as absent, reasoning that
// `par_map` is fork-join over a *known* task set and so cannot express an
// unbounded accept loop. The first half is right; the conclusion was not.
//
// A worker pool does not need the task set to be the *requests*. It needs it
// to be the **workers**, and each worker to find its own work. Since a socket
// descriptor is an `Int`, the shared listener is an ordinary `par_map` task
// value: hand the same listener to N workers and let each one race `accept`
// on it, exactly as `concurrent-worker`'s `drain_queue` races `chan_recv` on
// a shared channel. The kernel's accept queue *is* the work queue, so no
// channel is needed to distribute connections — the OS already does it.
//
// What stays true is the boundary: this is structured fork-join, so the pool
// serves a **bounded** number of connections and joins before returning. A
// server that runs forever is a different shape (it wants detached spawn,
// which v0 deliberately does not have), and this does not pretend otherwise —
// it takes a connection budget and returns when the budget is spent.

/// How many connections each worker in the pool serves before it joins.
/// Together with `pool_workers` this bounds the pool: fork-join must join,
/// so the work has to be finite.
fn per_worker_budget() -> Int {
    2
}

/// The pool's width — the number of OS threads `par_map` forks.
fn pool_workers() -> Int {
    4
}

/// How many consecutive idle intervals a pool worker tolerates before
/// retiring.
///
/// Small on purpose: fork-join cannot return until every worker joins, so an
/// idle worker delays the whole pool's exit. Three strikes at
/// `pool_idle_ms()` tolerates an ordinary gap between bursts without letting
/// a worker linger.
fn pool_idle_strike_limit() -> Int {
    3
}

/// EFFECT: one pool worker — accept and serve `per_worker_budget()`
/// connections off the **shared** listener, returning how many it served.
///
/// Every worker receives the same listener descriptor and races the others to
/// `accept` it. Which worker wins any particular connection is
/// scheduler-dependent and deliberately unobservable; the *total* served
/// across the pool is not, which is what makes the pool testable at all. A
/// worker whose accept times out stops early rather than blocking the join.
///
/// Effectful, so no spec (`R0007`); pinned natively by the dogfood test.
/// How long a pool worker waits for a connection before concluding there is
/// no more work and joining.
///
/// Deliberately shorter than `io_timeout_ms`, and for a different reason: that
/// bound protects an *in-progress* request from a peer that stalls mid-write,
/// where waiting is the correct behavior. This one bounds the pool's *idle*
/// time, where waiting achieves nothing — fork-join cannot return until every
/// worker joins, so an unused worker sitting on a long accept delays the whole
/// pool's exit by that much. Separating the two means a slow client is still
/// tolerated while an idle pool still shuts down briskly.
fn pool_idle_ms() -> Int {
    1000
}

fn pool_worker(take listener: Int) -> Int {
    var served = 0;
    var remaining = per_worker_budget();
    var idle_strikes = 0;
    while remaining > 0 {
        let conn = std::rt::accept_timeout(listener, pool_idle_ms());
        if conn < 0 {
            // A timeout means no client has arrived *yet*; a host error means
            // the listener is broken. Retiring on the first quiet interval
            // conflated the two, so one 1-second gap between bursts could
            // retire a worker — and with four workers racing the same accept
            // queue, a bursty client could retire the whole pool before its
            // budget was spent, leaving later connections unanswered.
            //
            // `serve_on` already had this distinction (`idle_strike_limit`)
            // and `serve_forever` uses `is_timeout`; the pool is now
            // consistent with both, while still joining promptly — the strike
            // limit is small precisely because an idle worker delays the
            // fork-join's return.
            if !is_timeout(conn) {
                remaining = 0;
            } else {
                idle_strikes = idle_strikes + 1;
                if idle_strikes >= pool_idle_strike_limit() {
                    remaining = 0;
                }
            }
        } else {
            idle_strikes = 0;
            let status = serve_once(conn);
            if status > 0 {
                served = served + 1;
            }
            remaining = remaining - 1;
        }
    }
    served
}

/// EFFECT: serve on `port` with a real **concurrent worker pool** — fork
/// `pool_workers()` OS threads over the shared listener, each serving its own
/// budget of connections, and join. Returns the total served.
///
/// The capacity is `pool_workers() * per_worker_budget()` connections, served
/// concurrently rather than one at a time: several requests can be in flight
/// on different threads, which is the property `serve_on`'s serial loop lacks
/// and the reason a pool is worth having.
///
/// No data race is expressible here by construction, which is ADR-0007's
/// guarantee doing real work: the workers share only the listener descriptor,
/// an `Int` the kernel synchronizes internally, and every connection is owned
/// by exactly one worker from `accept` to `close`. The router itself is pure,
/// so there is no shared mutable state for the threads to contend over — a
/// property worth noticing, because it is what makes a routing table safe to
/// serve from N threads without a single lock.
///
/// Effectful, so no spec (`R0007`); pinned natively by the dogfood test.
fn serve_pool(take port: Int) -> Int {
    let listener = std::rt::listen(port);
    if listener < 0 {
        return 0 - 1;
    }
    // The task set is the WORKERS, not the requests: each entry is the same
    // shared listener, and each worker finds its own connections on it.
    var workers = std::array::empty();
    var w = 0;
    while w < pool_workers() {
        std::array::push(workers, listener);
        w = w + 1;
    }
    let served = std::rt::par_map(pool_worker, workers, pool_workers());
    var total = 0;
    var i = 0;
    while i < std::array::len(served) {
        total = total + std::array::get(served, i);
        i = i + 1;
    }
    std::rt::close(listener);
    total
}

/// EFFECT: read argument `i` as a non-negative decimal integer, or -1 when it
/// is absent or malformed.
///
/// The digits are read straight off `std::rt::arg_byte` (ADR-0013) rather than
/// through `std::str::parse_int`, because this example is a standalone file:
/// it consumes no stdlib module, so it decodes the few bytes it needs itself.
///
/// Effectful, so no spec (`R0007`); pinned natively by the dogfood test.
fn arg_int(take i: Int) -> Int {
    if i >= std::rt::arg_count() {
        return 0 - 1;
    }
    var value = 0;
    var j = 0;
    var digits = 0;
    var bad = false;
    var scanning = true;
    while scanning {
        let b = std::rt::arg_byte(i, j);
        if b < 0 {
            scanning = false;
        } else {
            if b < 48 || b > 57 {
                bad = true;
                scanning = false;
            } else {
                value = value * 10 + (b - 48);
                digits = digits + 1;
                j = j + 1;
            }
        }
    }
    if bad || digits == 0 {
        0 - 1
    } else {
        value
    }
}

/// Three modes, selected by the command line:
///
///   * `router` — the self-test: serve the six sample requests to itself over
///     loopback and exit 74 only when the wire agrees with the pure table.
///   * `router <port> <n>` — serve `n` real connections serially on `port`.
///   * `router <port> 0` — serve on `port` with the concurrent worker pool.
///
/// The two serving modes exit with the number of connections served, which is
/// what lets the dogfood tests assert the server did the work rather than
/// merely surviving.
fn main() -> Int {
    let port = arg_int(1);
    let count = arg_int(2);
    if port >= 0 && count >= 0 {
        // `router <port> 0` selects the concurrent pool, whose capacity is
        // fixed by `pool_workers` * `per_worker_budget` rather than passed in
        // — a serial run of zero connections would be a no-op anyway, so the
        // spelling is free.
        if count == 0 {
            return serve_pool(port);
        }
        // `router <port> 1` with a third argument selects the unbounded
        // serial server, which never returns. The budgeted form stays the
        // default because every test drives it: a run that cannot finish is
        // useless as an acceptance oracle, so `serve_forever` has to be
        // opted into explicitly.
        if std::rt::arg_count() > 3 {
            return serve_forever(port);
        }
        return serve_on(port, count);
    }
    let pure_report = report();
    if live_report() != pure_report {
        return 0;
    }
    std::rt::write(1, "router: 6 requests served over loopback, wire == table\n");
    pure_report
}

// --- Executable specs -------------------------------------------------------

spec method_tag {
    then method_tag("GET") == method_get();
    then method_tag("POST") == method_post();
    then method_tag("DELETE") == method_delete();
    then method_tag("PATCH") == 0;
    then method_tag("") == 0;
    // Decoding is exact, not a prefix match.
    then method_tag("GETX") == 0;
}

spec slot_for {
    then slot_for(routes(), "/") == 0;
    then slot_for(routes(), "/health") == 1;
    then slot_for(routes(), "/users") == 2;
    // An unregistered path resolves to the fallback slot, never a stale hit.
    then slot_for(routes(), "/missing") == not_found_slot();
    then slot_for(routes(), "") == not_found_slot();
    // Paths are matched exactly: no prefix or suffix match leaks through.
    then slot_for(routes(), "/users/1") == not_found_slot();
    then slot_for(routes(), "health") == not_found_slot();
}

spec "the split table's two halves agree" {
    // The load-bearing invariant v0 cannot state in a type: every registered
    // path must resolve to a slot that is in range, and the table must hold
    // exactly `route_count()` handlers. If a route were registered with a slot
    // past the end, `dispatch` would trap on the bounds check at runtime.
    then slot_for(routes(), "/") < route_count();
    then slot_for(routes(), "/health") < route_count();
    then slot_for(routes(), "/users") < route_count();
    then not_found_slot() < route_count();
    then std::map::len(routes()) == route_count() - 1;
}

spec "every route dispatches to its own handler" {
    // Each registered path reaches the handler that path is *for* — the
    // check that catches a registration whose slot drifted from
    // `handler_table`'s order, which is the split table's real failure mode.
    then status_for("GET", "/") == status_ok();
    then status_for("GET", "/health") == status_no_content();
    then status_for("GET", "/users") == status_ok();
    // Distinct handlers really are distinct: `/` and `/health` both answer a
    // GET, but with different statuses, so a swapped registration is visible.
    then status_for("GET", "/") != status_for("GET", "/health");
}

spec "handlers receive the request, not just the path" {
    // Same path, different method, different status: proof the handler is
    // called with the request rather than selected by path alone.
    then status_for("POST", "/users") == status_created();
    then status_for("GET", "/users") == status_ok();
    then status_for("POST", "/users") != status_for("GET", "/users");
}

spec "an unmatched path dispatches to the fallback handler" {
    then status_for("GET", "/missing") == status_not_found();
    then status_for("POST", "/nope") == status_not_found();
    // The fallback answers regardless of method — 404 precedes any method
    // policy, because an unknown path has no policy to consult.
    then status_for("DELETE", "/missing") == status_not_found();
    then status_for("PATCH", "/missing") == status_not_found();
}

spec "a known path refuses a method it does not allow" {
    // 405, not 404: the path exists, the method is wrong. Getting this
    // backwards is the classic router bug, so it is pinned explicitly.
    then status_for("DELETE", "/health") == status_method_not_allowed();
    then status_for("POST", "/health") == status_method_not_allowed();
    then status_for("POST", "/") == status_method_not_allowed();
    // An unknown method on a known path is refused, not dispatched.
    then status_for("PATCH", "/users") == status_method_not_allowed();
}

spec handle_not_found {
    // The fallback is total: every method reaches the same answer.
    then handle_not_found(Request { method: method_get(), path: "/x", query: "", param: "" }).status == status_not_found();
    then handle_not_found(Request { method: 0, path: "", query: "", param: "" }).status == status_not_found();
    // …and it answers with a body, so a client learns why, not just that.
    then std::str::len(std::string::as_str(handle_not_found(Request { method: 0, path: "", query: "", param: "" }).body)) > 0;
}

spec int_to_text {
    // Content-Length is computed now, so the renderer that produces it is
    // pinned across the boundaries a hand-rolled loop gets wrong.
    then std::string::as_str(int_to_text(0)) == "0";
    then std::string::as_str(int_to_text(7)) == "7";
    then std::string::as_str(int_to_text(10)) == "10";
    then std::string::as_str(int_to_text(16)) == "16";
    then std::string::as_str(int_to_text(99)) == "99";
    then std::string::as_str(int_to_text(100)) == "100";
    then std::string::as_str(int_to_text(1024)) == "1024";
}

spec headers_for {
    // The measured length reaches the wire. A wrong Content-Length is worse
    // than a missing body: the client hangs waiting for bytes that never
    // come, or truncates the ones it got.
    then std::string::as_str(headers_for(0, false)) == "\r\nContent-Length: 0\r\nConnection: close\r\n\r\n";
    then std::string::as_str(headers_for(16, false)) == "\r\nContent-Length: 16\r\nConnection: close\r\n\r\n";
    // Whatever the length, the block still terminates with a blank line.
    then std::str::slice(std::string::as_str(headers_for(5, false)), std::str::len(std::string::as_str(headers_for(5, false))) - 4, std::str::len(std::string::as_str(headers_for(5, false)))) == "\r\n\r\n";
}

spec "handlers answer with bodies, and 204 answers without one" {
    // The body is real: routes that carry content report a non-zero length,
    // measured through the same accessor the wire layer uses.
    then body_len_for("GET", "/") > 0;
    then body_len_for("GET", "/users") > 0;
    then body_len_for("POST", "/users") > 0;
    then body_len_for("GET", "/missing") > 0;
    // 204 is defined to carry no content, so this route must stay empty —
    // the case that proves the body is genuinely optional rather than always
    // present and sometimes ignored.
    then body_len_for("GET", "/health") == 0;
    // A refused method answers with a status and no body.
    then body_len_for("DELETE", "/health") == 0;
    // Distinct routes really carry distinct content, so a body cannot be a
    // constant that happens to look right.
    then std::string::as_str(route("GET", "/").body) != std::string::as_str(route("GET", "/users").body);
    // GET and POST on the same path differ in body as well as status.
    then std::string::as_str(route("GET", "/users").body) != std::string::as_str(route("POST", "/users").body);
}

spec response_line {
    // The reverse of the table: every status the router can answer with has a
    // distinct status line, so a client can read back what was routed.
    then response_line(status_ok()) == "HTTP/1.1 200 OK";
    then response_line(status_created()) == "HTTP/1.1 201 Created";
    then response_line(status_no_content()) == "HTTP/1.1 204 No Content";
    then response_line(status_not_found()) == "HTTP/1.1 404 Not Found";
    then response_line(status_method_not_allowed()) == "HTTP/1.1 405 Method Not Allowed";
    // Distinct statuses stay distinct on the wire — a collision here would
    // make two different outcomes indistinguishable to a client.
    then response_line(status_ok()) != response_line(status_no_content());
    then response_line(status_not_found()) != response_line(status_method_not_allowed());
}

spec find_space {
    // The request line splits on spaces: "GET /health HTTP/1.1".
    then find_space("GET /health HTTP/1.1", 0) == 3;
    then find_space("GET /health HTTP/1.1", 4) == 11;
    // A line with no space at all reports -1 rather than a bogus index, which
    // is what routes malformed input to the fallback instead of trapping.
    then find_space("garbage", 0) == 0 - 1;
    then find_space("", 0) == 0 - 1;
    // Searching past the last space finds nothing.
    then find_space("GET /", 5) == 0 - 1;
}

spec report {
    then report() == 74;
    // The report fits in one byte, so the process exit status carries it
    // exactly — no truncation for a reader to account for.
    then report() < 256;
    then report() > 0;
}

spec "query and path splitting" {
    // A target with no query is a path, unchanged.
    then target_path("/users") == "/users";
    then target_query("/users") == "";
    then query_start("/users") == 0 - 1;
    // A query splits off at the first `?` and is never part of the key.
    then target_path("/users?page=2") == "/users";
    then target_query("/users?page=2") == "page=2";
    // A bare `?` yields an empty query, not a missing one.
    then target_path("/users?") == "/users";
    then target_query("/users?") == "";
}

spec query_get {
    then query_get("page=2", "page") == "2";
    then query_get("a=1&page=2&b=3", "page") == "2";
    then query_get("a=1&page=2", "a") == "1";
    // An absent key, and a key that is a prefix of a present one, both miss.
    then query_get("a=1&page=2", "missing") == "";
    then query_get("pages=9", "page") == "";
    // A key with no value is present but empty.
    then query_get("page=", "page") == "";
    then query_get("", "page") == "";
    // The first of a repeated key wins.
    then query_get("page=1&page=2", "page") == "1";
}

spec "path parameters" {
    then parent_path("/users/42") == "/users";
    then last_segment("/users/42") == "42";
    // A top-level path has no parent segment to strip.
    then parent_path("/users") == "";
    then parent_path("/") == "";
    then last_segment("/users") == "users";
}

spec "parameterized routes reach their parent handler" {
    // `/users/42` must reach the `/users` handler rather than 404 — the
    // routing behavior item 2 adds, stated as the property rather than the
    // mechanism.
    then status_for("GET", "/users/42") == status_ok();
    then status_for("GET", "/users?page=2") == status_ok();
    // A literal route still wins, and an unknown parent still 404s.
    then status_for("GET", "/users") == status_ok();
    then status_for("GET", "/nope/42") == status_not_found();
    // The parameter and the query actually reach the handler: each answers
    // with a body distinct from the unfiltered list.
    then body_len_for("GET", "/users/42") != body_len_for("GET", "/users");
    then body_len_for("GET", "/users?page=2") != body_len_for("GET", "/users");
    // A method the route refuses is still refused with a parameter present.
    then status_for("PATCH", "/users/42") == status_method_not_allowed();
}

spec "header parsing" {
    // Header names are case-insensitive, which is the property that keeps a
    // real client's `content-length:` from being missed.
    then header_is("Content-Length: 11", "Content-Length");
    then header_is("content-length: 11", "Content-Length");
    then header_is("CONTENT-LENGTH: 11", "Content-Length");
    then !header_is("Content-Type: text/plain", "Content-Length");
    // A name must be followed by a colon, not merely be a prefix.
    then !header_is("Content-Lengths: 11", "Content-Length");
    then !header_is("Content-Length", "Content-Length");
}

spec header_int {
    then header_int("Content-Length: 11", "Content-Length") == 11;
    then header_int("content-length:11", "Content-Length") == 11;
    then header_int("Content-Length:   0", "Content-Length") == 0;
    // A non-numeric or absent value is -1, distinct from a genuine zero.
    then header_int("Content-Length: abc", "Content-Length") == 0 - 1;
    then header_int("Content-Length: ", "Content-Length") == 0 - 1;
    then header_int("Content-Type: text/plain", "Content-Length") == 0 - 1;
}

spec "keep alive headers" {
    // The `Connection:` header must say what the server will actually do.
    then std::string::as_str(headers_for(16, true)) == "\r\nContent-Length: 16\r\nConnection: keep-alive\r\n\r\n";
    then std::string::as_str(headers_for(16, false)) == "\r\nContent-Length: 16\r\nConnection: close\r\n\r\n";
}

spec is_timeout {
    // -3 is "not yet", -2 is "not ever": a server that conflates them either
    // exits on a quiet second or spins on a broken listener.
    then is_timeout(0 - 3);
    then !is_timeout(0 - 2);
    then !is_timeout(0 - 1);
    then !is_timeout(0);
}

spec "a hostile Content-Length cannot trap the parser" {
    // The regression that matters most: tuonelang traps on integer overflow
    // and a trap aborts the PROCESS, so an unbounded accumulate let any peer
    // kill the server with one header line. Each of these used to overflow on
    // its way to being rejected; all must now return -1 without trapping.
    then header_int("Content-Length: 99999999999999999999", "Content-Length") == 0 - 1;
    then header_int("Content-Length: 123456789012345678901234567890", "Content-Length") == 0 - 1;
    // Bad bytes after a huge run of digits: the old loop kept scanning past
    // its own `bad` flag, so the rejection came too late to prevent the trap.
    then header_int("Content-Length: 99999999999999999999abc", "Content-Length") == 0 - 1;
    // A value just over the ceiling is refused; the ceiling itself is fine.
    then header_int("Content-Length: 8388609", "Content-Length") == 0 - 1;
    then header_int("Content-Length: 8388608", "Content-Length") == max_body_bytes();
    // Ordinary values are unaffected by the ceiling check.
    then header_int("Content-Length: 0", "Content-Length") == 0;
    then header_int("Content-Length: 11", "Content-Length") == 11;
    then header_int("Content-Length: 1048576", "Content-Length") == 1048576;
}
